<main class="layout-container layout-stack-main">

  <header class="surface-bento page-hero-bento">
    <h1 class="text-page-title">Pre-1978 Lead &amp; Pre-1980 Asbestos Disclosures</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      Navigating historic building materials across King and Snohomish counties: understanding NWMLS Form 22J, PSCAA renovation rules, encapsulated materials, and practical buyer strategies.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">Why Surface Testing During Escrow Is Often Counterproductive</h2>
      <p class="text-body">
        I generally do <strong>not</strong> recommend ordering surface lead or asbestos testing during a quick home purchase inspection window. With homes built prior to 1978 or 1980, lead paint can easily be buried under 20 layers of modern paint, or asbestos might exist in one hidden pipe elbow while testing negative everywhere else. Surface tests frequently produce false negatives or create unnecessary panic over completely intact, encapsulated materials.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body">
        Unless you're planning an immediate, uncontained renovation, intact paint and undisturbed materials pose minimal active risk. If lead or asbestos is a strict deal-breaker for your household, the simplest and safest strategy is focusing your search on homes built in <strong>1980 or later</strong>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Core Statutory Frameworks</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Federal and state regulations treat lead and asbestos as two distinct health risks governed by separate disclosure and testing bodies.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Pre-1978 Properties</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Lead-Based Paint (Form 22J)</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="calc-field-group" style="margin-bottom: 0.85rem;">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Mandatory Disclosure</span>
          <p class="text-body">Federal requirement executed via NWMLS Form 22J and delivery of the official EPA residential lead pamphlet.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group" style="margin-bottom: 0.85rem;">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Normal Living Risk</span>
          <p class="text-body">Extremely low if safely encapsulated under modern latex paint. Primary risk lies in friction surfaces (double-hung wooden window sashes, door jambs).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Remodeling Requirement</span>
          <p class="text-body">Subject to EPA RRP (Renovation, Repair, &amp; Painting) contractor guidelines requiring wet-sanding and dust containment.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Pre-1980 Properties</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Asbestos Building Materials</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="calc-field-group" style="margin-bottom: 0.85rem;">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Mandatory Disclosure</span>
          <p class="text-body">Declared strictly under Washington State Form 17 (Seller Disclosure Statement) Environmental section.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group" style="margin-bottom: 0.85rem;">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Normal Living Risk</span>
          <p class="text-body">Zero risk if intact and undisturbed (non-friable). Asbestos only poses a respiratory hazard when fibers become airborne during breakage.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Remodeling Requirement</span>
          <p class="text-body">Subject to Puget Sound Clean Air Agency (PSCAA) mandate requiring a certified asbestos survey prior to demolition.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Common Historic PNW Materials</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Asbestos was highly prized for fireproofing, insulation, and durability in Pacific Northwest homes built from the 1920s through the late 1970s.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Acoustic Ceilings</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Popcorn Spray Texture</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Heavy textured ceiling spray used aggressively from the 1950s through 1979 to hide drywall tape lines and dampen acoustic echo.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">HVAC Systems</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Gravity Furnace Duct Insulation</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          White paper tape ("snow tape") or thick corrugated cardboard wrapping applied to large basement heating trunks and ductwork elbows.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Basements &amp; Kitchens</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Vinyl Floor Tiles &amp; Mastic</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Rigid 9-inch by 9-inch vintage vinyl flooring tiles (Vct), frequently secured using dark black asphalt backing adhesives (mastic).
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Exterior Facades</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Cement Siding Shingles</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Incredibly durable, fire-resistant cementitious exterior wall tiles commonly installed on mid-century Seattle ramblers and split-levels.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Project Safety &amp; Action Matrix</h2>
    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-3">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Encapsulated Materials</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Living As-Is</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Intact, painted-over lead paint and undisturbed building materials pose minimal active health risk. Testing during a home purchase inspection is generally discouraged due to high false-negative rates on buried layers.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Paint &amp; Trim Work</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Cosmetic Repainting</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          For pre-1978 homes, ensure Form 22J is attached to your contract. When doing light cosmetic repairs, sanding door jambs, or repainting window sills, utilize EPA wet-sanding lead-safe practices to contain microscopic dust.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Demolition Boundaries</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Major Remodeling</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Prior to scraping popcorn ceilings, removing duct wrap, or tearing down walls in a pre-1980 home, Puget Sound Clean Air Agency (PSCAA) regulations strictly require a certified asbestos survey and professional abatement.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

